Le dimanche 17 mars 2024 à 17:00:18 UTC, Jürgen Niedballa a écrit :
> Hello,
> That is correct, it was removed it in the current version because we want to revisit the calculation and write a dedicated goodness-of-fit function for the next release which then correctly handles standard and Royle-Nichols occupancy models and accounts for the peculiarities of both JAGS and Nimblem But currently it it is not available since the priority was to get it back in .
> You can use release 2.2.1 on GitHub which still returns the bayesian p-values.
> Best regards,
> Jürgen
>
> On Fri, Mar 8, 2024, 07:50 Robin HORION <robin....@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> > Hello,
> >
> > I hope this message finds you well. I've been working with the communitymodel function in the R package, particularly following the guidelines outlined in the documentation here: https://jniedballa.github.io/camtrapR/articles/camtrapr5.html
> >
> > Up until a few weeks ago, the function was providing Bayesian p-values both overall and per species, as expected. However, recently, I've noticed a change in the output. The current output now includes species richness per station, and running the exact same code results in a different output. I have the outputs presented in R Markdown HTML format, making it clear that there is a difference, even when using identical code (see attached images).
> >
> >
> >
> >
> > I initially suspected a version mismatch as I've been working on different computers, but the issue persists across both systems. My main concern is that I can't seem to locate Bayesian p-values in the current output, and I'm unsure if I'm missing something obvious.
> >
> > Is there a way to switch back from species richness to Bayesian p-values, or am I overlooking a crucial step? I would greatly appreciate any guidance or insights you can provide.
> >
> > Thank you in advance for your time and assistance.
> >
> > Best regards,
> >
